# Example environment variables. Copy to .env.local and fill in.
# Only variables prefixed with VITE_ are exposed to the client bundle.
# NEVER put secrets in VITE_-prefixed variables — they ship to the browser.

# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# ── Clerk authentication ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only the PUBLISHABLE key belongs here. It is designed to ship in the client
# bundle and identifies the instance, nothing more.
#
# The SECRET key (sk_...) must never appear in this repo or in any VITE_-prefixed
# variable. Anything with that prefix is compiled into the bundle and is readable
# by every user of the app — and in a shipped desktop binary that is a full
# compromise of the Clerk instance: it can mint sessions, read every user, and
# delete all of them. `src/lib/auth-config.ts` throws on boot if it ever sees one,
# so the mistake fails on your machine rather than shipping quietly.
#
# Without this key MadMusic still runs. You lose cross-device sync; the
# catalogue, local folders and playback are unaffected.
#
# Get it from: Clerk dashboard -> your MadMusic app -> API keys -> Publishable key
# VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...

# ── Last.fm scrobbling (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Not VITE_-prefixed, and deliberately so. Last.fm signs every authenticated
# call with a SHARED SECRET, and anything with the VITE_ prefix is compiled into
# the JavaScript bundle where any user can read it from the devtools console.
# These are read by Rust at compile time instead — see
# src-tauri/src/scrobble.rs.
#
# Be honest about the limit: in a desktop app the secret ends up in the binary
# either way and can be extracted by anyone determined. That is true of every
# desktop scrobbler, and why Last.fm issues keys per application rather than per
# user. Keeping it out of the JS removes the easy path, not every path.
#
# Without these, scrobbling does not appear in Settings at all — a switch that
# can never work is worse than no switch. Everything else is unaffected.
#
# Get them from: https://www.last.fm/api/account/create
# MADMUSIC_LASTFM_KEY=...
# MADMUSIC_LASTFM_SECRET=...

# ── Convex backend (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The backend behind the social half of the app: profiles, follows, the activity
# feed, timed comments, collaborative playlists, listening sessions, uploads and
# cross-device sync. Everything else — the catalogue, local folders, playback,
# the library, playlists you keep to yourself — works with this unset.
#
# The deployment URL is public by design: it identifies the deployment and
# nothing more, and every function behind it checks the caller's Clerk token
# before it does anything. It is the one VITE_ variable here that is meant to
# ship.
#
# Set up with `pnpm convex`, which creates a deployment and writes this value.
# VITE_CONVEX_URL=https://your-deployment.convex.cloud
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing in through the browser, on desktop
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Where the *web* build of this app is hosted. The desktop app opens
# `<origin>/desktop-link` in the system browser, that page mints a short-lived
# sign-in ticket, and the app is handed it back through `madmusic://auth`.
#
# Unset, the desktop app simply does not offer "Continue in your browser" — a
# button with nowhere to send anybody is worse than no button. Signing in inside
# the app keeps working either way.
#
# The mint needs `CLERK_SECRET_KEY` on the Convex deployment, never here:
#   npx convex env set CLERK_SECRET_KEY sk_test_...
#
# See docs/auth-and-devices.md.
# VITE_WEB_ORIGIN=https://madmusic.example
#
# On the Convex side, tell it which Clerk instance to trust. This is set on the
# deployment rather than here:
#
#   npx convex env set CLERK_JWT_ISSUER_DOMAIN https://your-instance.clerk.accounts.dev
#
# The value is the Frontend API URL from the Clerk dashboard, and the JWT
# template must be named exactly `convex`. See convex/auth.config.ts.

# ── Metadata services (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# None of these are VITE_-prefixed: they are read by Rust at compile time, for
# the same reason the Last.fm secret is. See src-tauri/src/meta/.
#
# "At compile time" is worth taking literally: `option_env!` is resolved when
# cargo builds the crate, not when the app runs. `src-tauri/build.rs` reads this
# file and `.env.local` and hands the values to the compiler, so putting a key
# here is enough — but a key added *after* a build needs the crate rebuilt, and
# the build script asks cargo for that by watching both files.
#
# LRCLIB (lyrics), MusicBrainz, the Cover Art Archive, Wikidata and
# Radio-Browser need no key at all and are always on.
#
# Last.fm's PUBLIC key drives similar artists, charts and an artist's top
# tracks. It is the same key as MADMUSIC_LASTFM_KEY above and can be the same
# value; it is named separately because recommendations work without scrobbling
# being set up, and vice versa.
# Get it from: https://www.last.fm/api/account/create
# LASTFM_API_KEY=...
#
# Discogs adds release credits — engineers, producers, pressing details — where
# MusicBrainz has none. Without it, credits come from MusicBrainz alone.
# Get it from: https://www.discogs.com/settings/developers
# DISCOGS_TOKEN=...
#
# AcoustID identifies untagged files and recognises music playing nearby. It
# also needs the `fpcalc` binary, fetched by `pnpm fingerprinter`.
# Get it from: https://acoustid.org/new-application
# ACOUSTID_API_KEY=...
